{"bill":{"id":"119-hr-3863","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"3863","title":"VA Mental Health Outreach and Engagement Act","introducedDate":"2025-06-10","latestActionDate":"2026-05-04","latestActionText":"Placed on the Union Calendar, Calendar No. 550.","policyArea":"Armed Forces and National Security","subjects":["Congressional oversight","Government studies and investigations","Mental health","Veterans' medical care","Veterans' pensions and compensation"],"sponsorBioguideId":"B001315","cosponsorBioguideIds":["F000466","C001068","G000604"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"VA Mental Health Outreach and Engagement Act This bill requires the Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) to offer an annual mental health consultation to veterans who are receiving compensation for a service-connected disability relating to a mental health diagnosis. Additionally, the VA must conduct outreach regarding the availability of such consultations and other mental health services unless a veteran opts out of outreach. The bill requires the VA to utilize specified methods in its outreach efforts and to provide annual reports for the four years following the enactment of the bill regarding the outreach methods used to inform veterans about mental health consultations and services. The Government Accountability Office must report on the effect of this bill, including by reporting on the number of veterans who received mental health consultations and which outreach methods were most successful in reaching such veterans. The bill also extends certain higher loan fee rates through May 12, 2035, under the VA's home loan program. Such rates currently last through June 9, 2034.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/3863","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hr/BILLSTATUS-119hr3863.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:29:51.683Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hr-3863","plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of Veterans Affairs to offer annual mental health consultations to veterans receiving disability compensation for mental health conditions. The VA would also be required to conduct outreach about these consultations and other mental health services, using various methods and reporting on their effectiveness for four years. Additionally, the bill extends higher loan fees in the VA home loan program through May 2035, about one year longer than currently scheduled.","industries":[{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"The bill would increase demand for mental health consultation services provided to veterans, affecting providers who deliver VA mental health care."},{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The extension of higher VA loan fees through 2035 affects lenders who originate and service VA-guaranteed home loans."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Changes to VA home loan fee rates may affect the cost structure and volume of VA-backed mortgages used in home purchases."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"e29a9ee7ca8c7da4a57f8cb9","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:32:38.813Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would require the VA to offer an annual mental health consultation to veterans who are receiving compensation for a service-connected disability relating to a mental health diagnosis.","whoItTouches":"People getting help for their mental health. Veterans and their families.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in getting an appointment. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hr-3863","bioguideId":"F000466","cycle":2026,"score":0.0391161903606